WN.lb(5,{"mt_mB7DVai-Uf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How can you show a friend you are listening?","choices":["Look at them and keep still","Play with a toy car","Turn away and hum"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about Pip. What did Pip fix?","explain":"Looking at the speaker, a quiet mouth and still hands show you are listening."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two children talk at the same time. What happens?","choices":["Nobody can hear well","Everyone hears both","It is faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you hear two voices at once?","explain":"When two people talk at once, the words get mixed up, so take turns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ava says, \"My cat had {n} kittens!\" Which answer fits?","choices":["Wow! What colour are they?","I have a red ball.","Can we go out now?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer is about the kittens?","explain":"Asking about the {n} kittens shows you heard Ava's news.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You did not hear what your teacher said. What can you do?","choices":["Ask, \"Can you say it again?\"","Guess and hope","Do nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it OK to ask a grown-up to say it one more time?","explain":"It is fine to ask for it again. Guessing can go wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Dad says, \"Bring your blue hat.\" You bring your red hat. What did you miss?","choices":["The word blue","The word hat","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells you the colour?","explain":"You heard hat but missed blue. Every word matters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The teacher asks a question. You put your hand up. What next?","choices":["Wait until she says your name","Shout the answer","Stand on your chair"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who decides whose turn it is?","explain":"Hand up, then wait to be asked. That gives everyone a fair turn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your friend shows you {n} shells from the beach. Which answer fits?","choices":["They are so pretty! Where did you find them?","I want a drink.","Go away."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer talks about the shells?","explain":"Talking about the {n} shells shows you listened and care.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Grandpa is telling a story. You want to talk. What do you do?","choices":["Wait until he stops","Talk over him","Walk off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose turn is it right now?","explain":"It is Grandpa's turn. Wait for him to finish, then talk."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia is telling you about her trip. When is it your turn to talk?","choices":["When Mia has finished","Right now, while she talks","Never"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the talking ball. When did it move?","explain":"Wait until Mia stops. Then it is your turn, and you can both hear each other."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Gran says, \"Get {n} red apples.\" Which do you get?","choices":["{n} red apples","{n} green apples","One red apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen to every word: how many, and what colour?","explain":"Gran asked for {n} apples, and they must be red. Both little words matter.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Leo says, \"I fell off my bike.\" Which answer fits?","choices":["Oh no! Are you hurt?","I like pizza.","Look at my new shoes!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer is about what Leo said?","explain":"A good answer is about Leo's news. Asking if he is hurt shows you listened."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"At circle time, {n} children know the answer. What should they do?","choices":["Put their hands up and wait","All shout it out","Grab the talking ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can everyone get a turn?","explain":"With {n} children, hands up and waiting means one talks at a time and all can hear.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When a friend talks, you listen. Look at them. Keep your mouth quiet and your hands still.","Wait until they stop. Then it is your turn. If two people talk at once, nobody can hear.","Listen to every word. Gran says get the red cup. Not the blue cup. Little words matter.","Your answer should fit. Ava says she has a puppy. Ask, what is its name? Did you not hear? Say, can you say it again?"],"adult":["This topic is about turn-taking talk. A child listens while someone speaks, waits for a gap, and then replies to what was said. It matches the first spoken language aim of the English national curriculum: listen and respond to adults and peers. It also matches the early years goal of listening with care and replying with comments and questions that fit.","Young children often know the rule but find waiting hard. So the lab makes the wait visible. The speech bubble fills word by word, and the ball can only move once it says finished. Replies are checked in two ways. The child picks a picture that matches every word of a request, and picks an answer that is about a friend's news.","Common mistakes: calling out or talking over the speaker; giving an answer about yourself that ignores what was said; catching only the main word, so the red cup becomes any cup; fiddling with a toy while trying to listen; and nodding along instead of asking for a repeat.","At meals, pass a spoon round as a talking object, so only the holder speaks. When someone shares news, ask your child to say one thing back about it before they change the topic. Give two-part requests, such as get your blue shoes and your hat, and praise your child for getting both parts."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, pass a spoon round as the talking spoon. Only the person holding it talks. Everyone else looks at them and then says one thing back about what they said.","keyIdea":"Good listening has three parts. Wait until the speaker has finished. Show you are listening with your eyes, a quiet mouth and still hands. Then give an answer that fits what was just said. In a group, put your hand up and wait to be asked, so everyone gets a turn.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is this good listening or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good listening","Not listening"],"items":[["Look at the speaker",0],["Wait for your turn",0],["Ask a question about it",0],["Talk over them",1],["Play with a toy",1],["Change the topic",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglisten","caption":"Flagship lab: pass the talking ball when a friend finishes, help Pip listen, match pictures to every word, pick answers that fit, and put your hand up.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_n6GhzDPllD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which words help you wonder?","choices":["Maybe and what if","No and stop","Go away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words open up new ideas?","explain":"Maybe and what if invite new ideas. No and stop end the thinking."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why did the tower fall? Look: a dog is wagging its tail by the blocks.","choices":["Because the dog's tail hit it","Because towers are red","Because it fell down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the clue you can see.","explain":"The wagging tail is the clue. Red is not a reason, and fell down just says it again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which new word means very, very big?","choices":["Enormous","Tiny","Soggy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the huge orange ball.","explain":"Enormous means very, very big. Tiny means very small and soggy means wet and soft."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says, \"Let's build a boat.\" What adds to her idea?","choices":["Yes, and it can have a sail!","No, boats are silly.","I like cake."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer makes the boat idea bigger?","explain":"A sail adds to Mia's boat. No stops the idea, and cake is not about the boat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You put {n} ice cubes in the hot sun. What do you think will happen?","choices":["They melt","They stay ice","They turn into snow"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the ice test, what did the sun do?","explain":"The sun warms the {n} ice cubes, so they melt into water.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Leo thinks of {n} maybes for where the snowman went. Is that good thinking?","choices":["Yes, lots of ideas help","No, you should have one","No, you should say I don't know"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did more maybes help Pip with the puddle?","explain":"{n} maybes give Leo more to talk about and test, so he can pick the best one.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Gran reads the word soggy in a story. How can you make it your word?","choices":["Say it when you talk: my bread is soggy!","Never say it","Forget it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What helps a new word stick in your head?","explain":"Saying a new word out loud, in your own talk, helps you keep it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is the ice cream dripping? Which answer gives a reason?","choices":["Because the sun is hot","Because it is dripping","Because I like ice cream"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer says why, using a clue?","explain":"The hot sun melts the ice cream. The other answers do not say why it drips."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The puddle is gone. Pip says, \"Maybe the sun dried it.\" What should Pip do next?","choices":["Think of more maybes","Stop. One idea is enough","Say \"I don't know\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the thinking cloud. How many maybes did Pip get?","explain":"One idea is a start. More maybes, like the soil or a dog, help find the best one."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava has {n} ideas for the den. Leo says \"Yes, and we can add a torch!\" How many ideas now?","choices":["{=n+1}","{n}","{=n-1}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Leo take an idea away, or add one?","explain":"Yes, and adds a new idea, so {n} ideas become {=n+1}. Saying no would add none.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is Tom's coat wet? Which answer gives a reason?","choices":["Because it is raining","Because his coat is wet","Because it is Tuesday"],"answer":0,"hint":"A reason says why. Which one uses a clue?","explain":"Rain is a clue that explains the wet coat. Saying it is wet again does not say why."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Pip puts an ice cube in the freezer for {n} minutes. What happens?","choices":["It stays ice","It melts away","It gets bigger and bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ice test. Is the freezer warm or cold?","explain":"The freezer is colder than ice needs to stay solid, so after {n} minutes it is still ice.","params":{"n":[5,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Talk can help you think. When you do not know, say maybe. Maybe the sun dried the puddle. Maybe it sank into the mud. Think of lots of ideas, not just one.","Say why with because. Use a clue you can see. The coat is wet because it is raining.","Ask what if. What if we put the ice in the freezer? Then try it and see.","Try new words when you talk. A big ball can be enormous. When a friend has an idea, say yes, and add to it."],"adult":["This topic is exploratory talk: thinking out loud before settling on an answer. Children wonder about possibilities, give reasons, imagine what might happen, and try out new words they have heard. The English national curriculum asks pupils to use spoken language to develop understanding through speculating, hypothesising, imagining and exploring ideas, and to build vocabulary through talk.","The lab gives each move its own job. Several maybes go into a thinking cloud before any answer is chosen. A reason must use a clue in the picture. A what-if is tested with an ice cube in the sun, the shade and a freezer. New words are matched to pictures, and a den grows only when a reply adds to a friend's idea.","Common mistakes: stopping at the first idea or saying I don't know; giving a reason that just repeats the question, such as the coat is wet because it is wet; giving a reason that is not a clue at all; saying no to a friend's idea instead of building on it; and hearing a new word without ever saying it yourself.","Wonder aloud together on walks and at bath time. Ask what do you think and why, and accept several answers before testing one. Use story words in daily talk, and praise a child who adds to someone else's idea."]},"familyTip":"At bath time, ask \"What if we put this toy in the water?\" Let your child give two maybes, each with a because. Then drop it in and talk about what really happened.","keyIdea":"Talk helps you think. Say \"maybe\" and \"what if?\" to get lots of ideas, not just one. Give a reason with \"because\" and a clue you can see. Test a what-if to find out. Try new words out loud, and add to a friend's idea with \"yes, and\".","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help ideas grow, or stop them? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps ideas grow","Stops ideas"],"items":[["Maybe it…",0],["What if…?",0],["Yes, and…",0],["No.",1],["I don't know. Stop.",1],["That's silly.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwonder","caption":"Flagship lab: give Pip lots of maybes, pick a because from a clue, test what-ifs with an ice cube, try new words and grow a friend's idea.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_f8n4txtLej":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is it OK to ask a question when you do not know?","choices":["Yes, asking helps you learn","No, it means you are silly","No, you should always guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened when Pip guessed the red box?","explain":"Asking is how you learn. A guess can be wrong, like Pip and the red box."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Where is your coat?\" Which answer fits?","choices":["On the hook","My coat is red","Somewhere"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks where. Say a place.","explain":"On the hook says where. Red is true but about colour, and somewhere is too fuzzy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You can not reach the paints. What can you say?","choices":["Can you help me reach them, please?","I like paints","Give them now!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one asks for help in a kind way?","explain":"Can you help me, please? asks for help kindly, so someone can get the paints."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A word in the story is new to you. What can you ask?","choices":["What does that word mean?","Nothing, just skip it","Why is it raining?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pip asked about the word huge. What did Pip say?","explain":"Asking what a word means helps you learn it. Skipping it leaves you stuck."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"\"Do you want {n} grapes or {=n+2} grapes?\" Which answer fits?","choices":["{n} grapes, please","Yes","Grapes are green"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question gives two choices. Pick one.","explain":"It asks which one, so you say one choice: {n} grapes, please. Yes does not pick one.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is in the bag? Which question gives a clue?","choices":["Is it soft?","Is it my birthday?","Can I go out?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question is about the thing in the bag?","explain":"Is it soft? tells you about the thing. The others do not help find out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your friend talks very fast. You did not get it. What do you do?","choices":["Ask them to say it again","Nod and guess","Walk off"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you find out what they said?","explain":"Asking them to say it again lets you hear it. Nodding and guessing can go wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Gran asks, \"How many pancakes did you eat?\" You ate {n}. Which answer fits best?","choices":["I ate {n} pancakes","I ate","I like Gran"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gran asked how many. Say the number.","explain":"How many asks for a number, so say {n} pancakes. I ate does not say how many.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Pip did not hear where to sit. What should Pip do?","choices":["Ask where to sit","Sit anywhere and hope","Go home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Will a guess or a question get the real answer?","explain":"A question gets the real answer. A guess could put Pip in the wrong seat."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your friend asks, \"How many cars do you have?\" You have {n}. What do you say?","choices":["I have {n} cars","Cars are fast","Some"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question says how many. Give a number.","explain":"How many asks for a number, so the best answer is {n} cars.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You want to know what is in a box. Which question helps most?","choices":["What colour is it?","Is it bath time?","Do you like my shoes?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question gives a clue about the thing in the box?","explain":"What colour is it? gives a clue about the thing. The others are about something else."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The teacher said, \"Get {n} pens,\" but it was too fast. What is a kind way to ask?","choices":["Can you say that again, please?","WHAT?","Get any pens and hope"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one uses kind words?","explain":"Asking with please is kind, and then you hear it again: get {n} pens.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Did you not hear? Do you not know? Then ask! A guess can be wrong. A question finds out.","Some questions help a lot. What sound does it make? That is a good clue. Do you like hats? That does not help.","When a friend asks where the ball is, say where. Under the bed! Do not say I like balls.","Did it go too fast? Say: can you say that again, please? Then you will know. Asking is a smart thing to do."],"adult":["This topic is about asking and answering questions on purpose. A child asks when something is unclear or when they need more information, answers questions with relevant, specific detail, and asks for clarification politely, such as \"Can you explain that again?\"","The lab gives each part its own action. Children ask instead of guessing which box a block goes in, choose questions that give clues about a mystery box, drag answers that fit a friend's question, ask a fast-talking teacher to repeat kindly, and pick the question that gets help when Pip is stuck.","Common mistakes: guessing or copying a friend instead of asking; staying quiet because asking feels like admitting you are not clever; asking a question that does not help find out; answering a different question, or giving a fuzzy answer like somewhere or some; and asking in a cross way, such as shouting what.","At home, model questions out loud: \"I do not know how this works, so I will ask.\" Praise the question itself. When your child answers, gently ask for the exact detail: where, what or how many."]},"familyTip":"When you give your child a two-step job, stop and ask, \"Do you want me to say any part again?\" Thank them every time they ask a question, even if the answer seems obvious to you.","keyIdea":"When you do not know, ask. Do not guess. A good question gets a clue or the real answer. When someone asks you, say just what they asked. If you did not get it, ask again kindly: can you say that again, please?","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help you find out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps me find out","Does not help"],"items":[["Ask: Which one?",0],["Ask: Say it again, please?",0],["Ask: Can you help me?",0],["Guess and hope",1],["Say nothing",1],["Shout WHAT?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagask","caption":"Flagship lab: ask instead of guessing which box, ask good questions to open a mystery box, give answers that fit, ask again kindly and ask for help.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_LNYTNpJOGT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does it mean to build on someone's remark?","choices":["Keep their idea and add something new","Say their idea again in different words","Change the subject to your own idea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what makes a discussion move forward.","explain":"Building on a remark means linking to it and adding a reason, example, fact or question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Kai says, \"We should have {n} minutes of quiet reading each day.\" Which reply builds on it?","choices":["Yes, and quiet reading could help us calm down after lunch.","Yes, quiet reading every day.","Kai always wants to read."],"answer":0,"hint":"One reply adds a new reason. One repeats. One is about the person.","explain":"The first reply adds a new reason. Repeating adds nothing, and talking about Kai instead of the idea is not fair.","params":{"n":[10,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ella says, \"The library is always too noisy.\" Which follow-up question is best?","choices":["When is it the noisiest?","Do you like books?","What about the library and stuff?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question digs into Ella's exact point about noise?","explain":"Asking when it is noisiest builds on her point and could help solve it. The others are general or vague."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Max says, \"Phones should be allowed at school because I want mine.\" What is missing?","choices":["A real reason or example","A longer sentence","A question for Max"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is wanting something a strong reason?","explain":"Wanting something is not evidence. Max needs a reason or example, such as using a phone to check the bus time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A discussion about the class trip has drifted to pets. Which question steers it back?","choices":["Fun! So which place would we learn most from?","How many pets do you have?","Can we be quiet now?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a kind question that links to the main topic.","explain":"A friendly linking question brings the talk back to the trip. The others follow the drift or shut people down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which question is too vague to answer well?","choices":["What about things?","How long is the trip?","Why do you think the bus is late?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one does not say what it wants to know?","explain":"\"What about things?\" does not name anything, so nobody can answer it clearly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Noah says, \"Sports day should be in June.\" Ivy adds, \"Last year it rained {n} times in May but only once in June.\" What has Ivy done?","choices":["Backed up Noah's idea with evidence","Repeated Noah's idea","Changed the subject"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the rain remark a new fact about Noah's idea?","explain":"Ivy adds a fact that supports Noah's idea, so she builds on it with evidence.","params":{"n":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why are follow-up questions useful in a discussion?","choices":["They show you listened and help the speaker explain more","They let you change the subject","They make the talk end sooner"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens when you ask about what someone just said?","explain":"A follow-up question starts from what someone said, so it shows you listened and helps them say more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Grace says, \"We need more benches in the playground.\" Which reply is about the idea, not the person?","choices":["Yes, the old benches are always full at lunch.","Grace just wants to sit down.","Grace always complains."],"answer":0,"hint":"Talk about the idea, not the speaker.","explain":"Good replies respond to the idea. Comments about Grace herself do not help the discussion."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Zoe says, \"Our class should have a reading corner.\" Which reply builds on her idea?","choices":["Yes, and a comfy corner might get more of us reading at break.","Yes, our class should have a reading corner.","I read a comic last night."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply keeps Zoe's idea and adds something new?","explain":"The first reply keeps the reading corner idea and adds a new reason. The second only repeats it, and the third is off topic."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Omar says, \"{n} people in our class walk to school.\" Which follow-up question builds on what he said?","choices":["Would more people walk if there were a safe crossing?","What is your favourite colour?","What about stuff?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A follow-up starts from the speaker's own words.","explain":"The crossing question starts from Omar's point about walking and pushes it further. The others are off topic or too vague.","params":{"n":[6,14]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which question is the most specific?","choices":["How many minutes does the walk to school take?","What about the walk?","Is walking good or whatever?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could someone answer clearly?","explain":"A specific question names exactly what you want to know, here the number of minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Lily says, \"Recycling helps.\" Tom adds, \"Our school recycled {n} bags of paper last month.\" What did Tom add?","choices":["Evidence that backs up Lily's remark","A repeat of Lily's idea","An off-topic story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Tom's remark an opinion or a fact?","explain":"Tom gives a fact, the number of bags, which backs up Lily's remark. That is building with evidence.","params":{"n":[20,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A talk about the school garden drifts onto a TV show. What should you say?","choices":["That sounds fun, but what should we grow in the garden?","Which episode was the best?","Stop talking, this is boring."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply links back to the main question?","explain":"Being friendly and then asking a linking question steers the talk back without upsetting anyone."}],"explain":{"kid":["In a class discussion, everyone works on one question together. Your job is not just to say your own idea. It is to help the whole talk move forward.","The best way is to build on what someone said. Keep their idea and add something new. If Ava says a longer lunch gives more time to play, you could say, \"Yes, and running around might help us focus in the afternoon.\" Saying \"I agree, more time to play\" only repeats her.","You can also build with a follow-up question. Start from the speaker's exact words: \"Why do you think they run out of time?\" Make it specific. \"What about stuff?\" is too vague for anyone to answer. A fact or example makes a remark stronger, so back your ideas up.","Talk often drifts, one remark at a time, until nobody is on the question. You can steer it back kindly. Say something like, \"That's interesting, but how does it help us decide about lunch?\""],"adult":["This speaking and listening skill (ages 10 to 11, Common Core SL.5.1c) asks learners to pose and respond to specific questions and to make comments that contribute to a discussion and elaborate on the remarks of others. It is the step from taking turns to talking with each other, which researchers call exploratory or dialogic talk.","Elaborating means linking to a previous remark and adding to it: a reason, an example, evidence, a consequence or a respectful challenge. Useful sentence starters are \"Building on what Leo said...\", \"Can you say more about...?\" and \"I see it differently because...\". Follow-up questions that start from the speaker's words are the clearest sign that a learner was really listening.","Common mistakes: repeating a classmate's point in new words and calling it agreement; going off topic with a personal story; asking vague questions such as \"What about it?\"; giving opinions with no reason (\"because I want to\"); following a drift instead of steering back; and commenting on the person rather than the idea.","In the lab learners choose a reply that builds rather than repeats, ask two follow-up questions, link facts to the remarks they support, steer a drifting discussion back with a linking question, and sort vague and specific questions. At home, try the \"yes, and\" game at dinner: each person must add something new to the last idea."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, pick a question like \"Should kids get pocket money?\" Take turns, but each person must start with \"Building on what you said...\" and add a reason, example or follow-up question. If the talk drifts, anyone can ask a question that brings it back.","keyIdea":"A good discussion grows. Instead of repeating what someone said, you build on it: keep their idea and add something new, such as a reason, an example or a fact. Ask follow-up questions about what the speaker actually said, keep questions specific, and if the talk drifts, ask a question that links back to the main topic.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Ava says, \"A longer lunch would give us more time to play.\" Which replies build on her idea? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Builds on it","Does not build"],"items":[["Yes, and play helps us focus later.",0],["Why do you think play helps?",0],["Last week we had no time to play.",0],["I agree, more time to play.",1],["I like pizza.",1],["What about stuff?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbuildon","caption":"Flagship lab: build on a reply, ask follow-up questions, back remarks with evidence, steer drifting talk back on track and sharpen vague questions.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sXRHr7tfS5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a hook at the start of a talk?","choices":["Something that makes people want to listen","The title of your talk","The last thing you say"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of how a fishing hook catches a fish.","explain":"A hook catches your listeners: a question, a surprising fact or a short story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why should you look at your audience, not just your notes?","choices":["They feel you are talking to them","It makes you talk faster","So you can skip parts"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does it feel when someone talks to you without looking up?","explain":"Eye contact makes listeners feel included, and it lets you check their faces."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A friend looks puzzled during your talk. What do you do?","choices":["Say it again with an example","Carry on faster","Ignore it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A puzzled face means they did not follow.","explain":"Say it again with an example, so they can follow before you move on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which reply has a reason?","choices":["\"I agree with Sam because we would have more time to read.\"","\"I agree.\"","\"Same as Sam.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word 'because'.","explain":"\"I agree with Sam because…\" gives a reason. The others just say yes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} people have shared ideas. You want to add on to the last one. Which start fits?","choices":["\"I'd like to add that…\"","\"You're wrong.\"","\"Whatever.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Adding on builds on what someone said.","explain":"\"I'd like to add that…\" builds on the last of the {n} ideas instead of knocking it down.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You talk for {n} minutes at a steady pace. Is it better to talk very fast and finish sooner?","choices":["No, listeners lose track","Yes, they like it short","Yes, fast sounds exciting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can people follow if you rush?","explain":"No. A steady pace for {n} minutes beats a rushed talk that nobody can follow.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which line is best for the start of a class talk?","choices":["\"Good morning, everyone. Today I will tell you about bees.\"","\"Hiya! Bees, yeah?\"","\"Oi, listen up!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"A class talk uses formal, polite language.","explain":"\"Good morning, everyone…\" is formal and polite, which fits a talk to the class."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the best way to start a talk about sharks?","choices":["\"Did you know some sharks never stop swimming?\"","\"Um, this is about sharks.\"","\"I don't really know much about sharks.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes you want to hear more?","explain":"A surprising fact as a question is a hook. It makes the class want to listen."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} children in the back row. They cup their ears. What should you do?","choices":["Speak up, clearly","Talk faster","Look at your notes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cupping an ear is a sign. What does it tell you?","explain":"Speak up, clearly. The {n} children at the back cannot hear you, so faster or quieter will not help.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Priya says \"Hamsters make great class pets.\" Which reply values her view?","choices":["\"I disagree, because they sleep all day.\"","\"That's a silly idea, Priya.\"","\"No.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Talk about the idea, and give a reason.","explain":"\"I disagree, because they sleep all day.\" It is about her idea and gives a reason. Calling her silly is about the person."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your talk lasts {n} minutes. Halfway, {k} children yawn. What should you do?","choices":["Ask the class a question","Keep reading your notes","Speed up to finish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yawning means they have drifted. How can you bring them back?","explain":"Ask the class a question. After about {=n/2} minutes, {k} yawns mean you need to bring them back in.","params":{"n":[4,10],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n % 2 == 0"}],"explain":{"kid":["When you give a talk, you want people to listen. Start with a hook: a surprising fact or a question.","Speak at a steady pace. Not too fast, not too slow. Speak up so the back row can hear. Look at the people, not just your notes.","Keep checking faces. A puzzled face? Say it again with an example. A yawn? Ask a question. A hand up? Stop and let them ask.","When a friend shares an idea, you can agree, disagree or add on. Always say why: 'I agree with Priya because…' Disagree with the idea, never the person."],"adult":["The National Curriculum for spoken language (years 1 to 6) asks pupils to gain, maintain and monitor the interest of the listener, to consider and evaluate different viewpoints by attending to and building on the contributions of others, and to select and use appropriate registers. At seven and eight this shows up in show-and-tell, class talks and group discussion.","Engaging listeners has three parts. Gain: a hook such as a question or a surprising fact. Maintain: steady pace, clear volume, varied tone, and eye contact rather than reading from notes. Monitor: noticing puzzled, bored or lost faces and changing course, for example by giving an example or asking the audience a question.","Valuing viewpoints means replying to what someone actually said. Sentence stems help: 'I agree with … because', 'I disagree because', 'I'd like to add'. Common mistakes are a bare 'I disagree' with no reason, a reason that does not fit the stance, and criticising the person instead of the idea. Register is the last strand: formal language for a class talk, informal language with friends.","In the lab your child picks a hook and watches a class's attention, drags pace and volume dials and looks up from the notes until every listener smiles, reads four listener signs and picks the fix, builds agree, disagree and add-on replies to Priya with fitting reasons, and sorts lines into class talk or chatting with friends."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, let your child give a one-minute talk about their day. Before they start, ask: 'What is your hook?' Afterwards, share a view and ask them to reply with 'I agree because…' or 'I'd add…'.","keyIdea":"A good speaker wins the listeners' interest, keeps it and checks it. Start with a hook, speak at a steady pace and a clear volume, and look at your audience. Watch their faces and change what you do. When someone shares a view, agree, disagree or add on, and always give a reason. Use formal words for a class talk and informal words with friends.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help listeners? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Helps listeners","✗ Loses listeners"],"items":[["Start with a question",0],["Look at your audience",0],["Speak up so the back row can hear",0],["Reply with a reason: 'I agree because…'",0],["Read your notes facing the board",1],["Talk really fast to finish",1],["Say 'That's a silly idea'",1],["Ignore a hand that goes up",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagengage","caption":"Flagship lab: hook a class, set pace, volume and eye contact, read listeners' faces, reply to Priya with reasons, and pick the right register.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iodbOOmEQs":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In a talk, what is the point?","choices":["What the speaker wants you to agree with","A fact with a number in it","The first word they say"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the big idea the whole talk is about.","explain":"The point (or claim) is what the speaker wants you to think or do. Reasons and evidence support it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"The library should open at lunch. According to Mrs Lee, {n} pupils asked to borrow books at lunch.\" Which part is evidence?","choices":["{n} pupils asked to borrow books at lunch","The library should open at lunch","According to"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence is a fact or example. \"According to\" only tells you where it comes from.","explain":"The fact about {n} pupils is the evidence, and Mrs Lee is the source. The first sentence is the point.","params":{"n":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Point: \"Our town needs a new park.\" Which is off the point?","choices":["Parks can be very old","Children have nowhere safe to play","The nearest park is 3 miles away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one does not help show the town needs a park?","explain":"\"Parks can be very old\" is about parks, but it does not show this town needs one. The other two are reasons and evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"Red is the nicest colour, so our team shirts should be red.\" What is wrong?","choices":["The reason is just an opinion","Too few examples","There is no reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can \"nicest colour\" be proved?","explain":"\"Nicest colour\" is an opinion, not evidence. People can disagree, so it does not prove the point."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"\"It rained on sports day, so we should stop doing PE.\" What is wrong?","choices":["The point does not follow from the reason","It has evidence from a survey","It is a strong argument"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say \"because\" between them. Does it make sense?","explain":"One rainy sports day has nothing to do with stopping PE. The point does not follow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A speaker says: \"Video games make children unkind.\" Which follow-up question tests the reasoning best?","choices":["What evidence supports that?","What is your favourite game?","Can you say it again?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good question checks the reason or the evidence.","explain":"\"What evidence supports that?\" asks the speaker to back up the claim. The others do not test it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"In our survey, {n} out of 30 pupils walk to school, so we need a safer crossing.\" Is this strong?","choices":["Yes: it has evidence that fits the point","No: it is just an opinion","No: it has no reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a fact? Is it about the point?","explain":"The survey gives a fact ({n} out of 30 walk), and many walkers is a fair reason for a safe crossing. That is strong.","params":{"n":[18,28]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"\"Our team lost because the pitch was wet.\" Which question asks about other explanations?","choices":["Could there be another reason you lost?","Was the pitch green?","Who scored first?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a question that asks whether the reason is the only one.","explain":"Asking for another reason tests whether the wet pitch really caused the loss. Maybe the other team played better."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"We should have a class pet because it would teach us to care for animals.\" What is the reason?","choices":["It would teach us to care for animals","We should have a class pet","Because"],"answer":0,"hint":"The reason answers \"why?\". Look after the word \"because\".","explain":"\"It would teach us to care for animals\" says why. \"We should have a class pet\" is the point. \"Because\" is the signal word."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A speaker says: \"For example, {n} children came to the book club last week.\" What is this?","choices":["Evidence","The main point","An opinion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it a fact or example, or what the speaker wants?","explain":"It is evidence: a real number ({n} children) that backs up a reason. \"For example\" signals it.","params":{"n":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Point: \"Our playground has only {n} bins. We need more bins.\" Which claim supports it?","choices":["Litter blows around because the bins are always full","Bins are often green","I like a tidy bedroom"],"answer":0,"hint":"The claim must help show we need more bins.","explain":"Full bins and blowing litter show there are not enough bins. Bin colour and a tidy bedroom are off the point.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"\"My friend got sick after eating cheese, so cheese is bad for everyone.\" What is wrong with the reasoning?","choices":["Too few examples","It has no point","Nothing, it gives a reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many people does the evidence come from?","explain":"One friend cannot show something is true for everyone. That is too few examples to support \"everyone\"."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which signal word usually brings in evidence?","choices":["For example","However","Firstly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one leads to a real case or fact?","explain":"\"For example\" brings in a real case that backs up a reason. \"Firstly\" often starts a reason, and \"however\" brings in a contrast."}],"explain":{"kid":["When people speak to persuade you, they make a point. A point is what they want you to think or do. \"We should plant a school garden\" is a point.","A good speaker gives reasons. A reason says why. \"A garden would help us learn science outside\" is a reason. Evidence backs up a reason with a fact, a number or a real example: \"In Year 3 we grew beans and measured them every day.\"","Signal words help you spot each part. \"Because\" and \"first\" often bring a reason. \"For example\" and \"according to\" often bring evidence. \"So\" often comes just before the point.","Then judge it. A reason must be about the point, not just the same topic. One or two examples are not enough to say \"everyone\". An opinion is not evidence. If you are not sure, ask: \"What evidence supports that?\" or \"Could there be another reason?\""],"adult":["This is a Speaking and Listening standard for ages 9 to 10 (Common Core SL.4.3): identify the reasons and evidence a speaker gives to support particular points. In the English National Curriculum it sits in spoken language. Children listen and respond, ask relevant questions and give clear explanations. Useful terms are point or claim, reason, evidence, signal words, relevant and logical.","Children first need to hear the shape of an argument: one point, a few reasons, and evidence for each reason. Listening is harder than reading, because spoken words are gone at once. Short talks help. So do signal words and a simple map: the point at the top, reasons below, and evidence under each reason.","Watch for common mistakes. Children call an example a reason. They treat any sentence on the same topic as support, such as \"I love lunch\" for a longer lunch break. They accept an opinion as evidence, or jump from one or two cases to \"everyone\". They miss a link that does not follow. A quick check: put \"because\" between the reason and the point. Does it still make sense?","In the lab your child sorts a short council talk into point, reasons and evidence. Then they tap signal words, decide which claims support a point, name what is wrong with weak reasoning, and pick the follow-up question that tests a claim."]},"familyTip":"Watch a short advert or listen to a news clip together. Ask: what do they want us to think? What reasons do they give? Is there any evidence, or just opinion? Take turns asking one good follow-up question.","keyIdea":"When someone speaks to persuade you, listen for three parts. The point (or claim) is what they want you to agree with. A reason says why. Evidence is a fact, number or example that backs up a reason. Then check: is each reason about the point, is the evidence enough, and does the point really follow?","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Point: \"Our school should have a longer lunch break.\" Is each line a reason or evidence? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💬 Reason (says why)","📊 Evidence (fact or example)"],"items":[["We need time to eat and still play",0],["Playing outside helps us focus in the afternoon",0],["The queue is so long we can't finish",0],["Last Monday, 40 children were still eating at 12:55",1],["In our survey, 9 out of 10 pupils said lunch is too short",1],["According to the cook, the queue takes 15 minutes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagreason","caption":"Flagship lab: sort a talk into point, reasons and evidence, tap signal words, test if claims support a point, judge reasoning and ask a probing question.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_S0hzjAeLSK":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word do we use to give a reason?","choices":["because","and then","the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"I think it is fun … it has a slide.","explain":"Because joins the opinion to the reason: I think it is fun because it has a slide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam says: I think frogs are cool because I just do. What is missing?","choices":["A real reason","An opinion","The word because"],"answer":0,"hint":"He says what he thinks. Does he say why?","explain":"I just do does not tell why, so a real reason is missing, like because they can jump so far."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"I think summer is the best because… Which reason fits?","choices":["I can swim in the sea.","my shoe is untied.","it is."],"answer":0,"hint":"The reason must be about summer and tell why.","explain":"Swimming in the sea is about summer and tells why. A shoe is off topic, and it is says nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Zoe says: I think carrots are yummy because they are crunchy. What is a kind way to disagree?","choices":["I disagree, because I like soft food more.","Carrots are yucky, you are wrong!","No."],"answer":0,"hint":"Be kind and give your own reason.","explain":"I disagree, because… is kind and gives a reason. You are wrong is not kind, and No gives no reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ben likes rain and Ella does not. Who is wrong?","choices":["Nobody: they can both have reasons","Ben","Ella"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this a fact or what each one thinks?","explain":"These are opinions. Ben and Ella can think different things and both give good reasons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ali gives {a} reasons for liking the zoo and {b} more reasons for liking the farm. How many reasons is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the zoo reasons and the farm reasons together.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b} reasons altogether.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"In a group of {n}, everyone gives an opinion and {k} children also say I agree with a reason. How many did not say I agree?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the children who agreed away from the group.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} children did not say I agree.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which ending fits a short opinion talk about pandas?","choices":["So pandas are my favourite!","Because I just do.","And then we had lunch."],"answer":0,"hint":"An ending says the opinion again with So.","explain":"So pandas are my favourite! closes the talk by saying the opinion again."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one gives an opinion and a reason?","choices":["I think snow is fun because I can make a snowman.","I think snow is fun.","Snow is fun because it is fun."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word because, and a reason that tells why.","explain":"It says what you think (snow is fun) and why (I can make a snowman). The others have no reason, or say the same thing again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children each say one opinion with one reason. How many times do you hear the word because?","answer":"n","hint":"Each child uses because once, to bring in the reason.","explain":"Each of the {n} children says because once, so you hear it {n} times.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"I like my teddy because… Which reason is the clearest?","choices":["he is soft and I cuddle him at night.","he is good.","I just do."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one helps you picture why the teddy is special?","explain":"Soft and cuddled at night tells us why. He is good says very little, and I just do is not a reason."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} children give an opinion. {k} of them forget to say why. How many gave a reason?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the children who gave no reason.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} children gave a reason.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An opinion is what you think. I think dogs are fun. Your friend may think cats are more fun. Both are fine.","Say why with the word because. I think dogs are fun because they play with me. The part after because is the reason.","Because I just do is not a reason. It does not tell why. Because it is good does not tell much. Say the thing you like: it has a funny dragon in it.","When a friend says what they think, you can agree or not. Be kind. Say I agree because, or I disagree because, and give your reason."],"adult":["This is spoken language in England's National Curriculum. Pupils should articulate and justify answers, arguments and opinions. They also take part in discussions, role play and debates. Development Matters asks children of three and four to express a point of view and to debate when they disagree. In Reception they connect one idea to another using connectives such as because. US Common Core asks kindergarten children to state an opinion or preference (W.K.1). Grade 1 adds a reason and some sense of closure (W.1.1).","The sentence stem I think … because … gives children the structure. The opinion comes first. The conjunction because brings in the reason. A longer turn can add a second reason with and, then close with So …. The stems I agree because and I disagree because teach children to answer the idea, not the person.","Common mistakes: an opinion with no reason, a circular reason (I like it because I like it, or because I just do), a reason that is true but off topic, a vague reason (it is good, it is nice), and disagreeing by attacking the speaker (you are wrong!) instead of giving a different reason. Children also often think that if two people disagree, one of them must be wrong; with opinions, both can have good reasons.","In the lab your child drags word cards into a speech bubble to build I think … because …, picks reasons that really explain, swaps vague reasons for clear ones, answers a friend kindly, and builds a short talk with two reasons and an ending."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, ask a which-do-you-like-best question (which pudding, which park, which book). When your child answers, smile and ask: why do you think that? Then share your own opinion with a because.","keyIdea":"An opinion is what you think or feel about something. To justify it, give a reason that tells why: I think … because …. A good reason is about the opinion (not off topic), says something new (not just I just do or it is good), and is clear enough for the listener to picture. Others may think differently; we can agree or disagree kindly and give our own reason.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"I think the park is fun because… Is it a real reason? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Real reason: tells why","❌ Not a reason"],"items":[["I can go on the swings",0],["I just do",1],["my friends play there",0],["it is Tuesday",1],["there is a big slide",0],["it is",1],["I can run on the grass",0],["my coat is blue",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagopine","caption":"Flagship lab: build I think … because … in a speech bubble, pick real and clear reasons, answer a friend kindly, and give a short opinion talk.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
